      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park, preserving ancestral lands, was officially designated in 1978.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://home.nps.gov/kaho/learn/historyculture/index.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Kaloko’s park preserves two fishponds, house platforms, petroglyphs, temples, graves, and historic trails.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://home.nps.gov/kaho/learn/historyculture/index.htm",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Spirit of Kaloko-Honokōhau report guided the park’s founding and ongoing stewardship.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://home.nps.gov/kaho/learn/historyculture/index.htm",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kaloko sits on Hawaii Island’s western coast, bordered by Kaiminani to the west and Holualoa to the south.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaloko%2C_Hawaii",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Legislation creating Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park sought to preserve traditional Hawaiian activities and showcase historic land use.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://home.nps.gov/kaho/learn/historyculture/index.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Spirit Report notes Kaloko’s resources represent an entire community’s historic site within the ahupua‘a.",
